":"adopted"}},{"id":"ARCHED-B100-20261007-021","qid":950021,"nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-021","relatedNodeIds":["REVIEW95-ARCHED-B100-20261007-021"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Peripteral temple","stem":"A peripteral Greek temple has which arrangement?","choices":["Colonnades at the front and rear only","Columns across the entrance front only","Two complete concentric colonnades around the cella","Half-columns attached to the exterior walls of the cella","A single colonnade running around all sides of the cella"],"correctIndex":4,"answer":"A single colonnade running around all sides of the cella","explanation":"“Peripteral” describes the surrounding colonnade. It is a plan arrangement, whereas Doric and Ionic identify architectural orders.","format":"single_best_answer","references":[{"file":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art — Architecture in Ancient Greece","verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/toah/hd/grarc/hd_grarc.htm","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"A04","title":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art — Architecture in Ancient Greece","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org/toah/hd/grarc/hd_grarc.htm","verification_status":"reference_supplied; see retrieval report","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"editorial-review-v100","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"previewOnly95":false,"seriesNumber95":21,"canonical_number":null,"revision95":1,"topic74":"Peripteral temple","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"B100:peripteral-temple","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["TOPIC:peripteral-temple"],"workIds95":[],"evidenceStatus95":"reference_only_pending_external_check","exactDuplicateOf95":[],"sourceSnapshotHash95":"9eedaec7c17862cb4d647f098ac6e9da16986c61f9a08037d3b4e9df6d5c35c9","fingerprint":"afaff6e34cbc6cd7a396","review100":{"date":"2026-10-09","scope":"Stem, five alternatives, correct 